IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `claq-common`

-- Civil Liability Act 2003 (Queensland) -- chapter 2 (liability for harm) and chapter 4, parts 1 and 1A (expressions
-- of regret and apologies), as the CLAIMANT meets them.
--
-- SEAT: the person who suffered harm and claims damages for it. "Can the court find a breach?", "Was I owed a warning?",
-- "Does an obvious, inherent or recreational risk defeat my claim?", "Can I sue the volunteer, the food donor, the
-- rescuer, the road authority?", "How much at least are my damages cut because I was drunk or relied on a drunk
-- driver?", "Can I get damages although I was committing an offence?", "Can I use the defendant's apology?".
--
-- Every judgment the Act leaves to the court ("foreseeable", "not insignificant", "a reasonable person would have",
-- "obvious to a reasonable person", "significant degree of risk", "just and equitable", "satisfied", "harshly and
-- unjustly") is asked as a fact and the answer is given conditionally on it; it is never decided here.
-- Each section answers only for the section; whether the claim succeeds overall is the general law of negligence,
-- which the Act does not codify (s 7(5)).
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Civil Liability Act 2003: liability for harm`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 1 div 1 -- s 9 breach of duty`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 9(1) "A person does not breach a duty to take precautions against a risk of harm unless-- (a) the risk was
-- foreseeable ...; and (b) the risk was not insignificant; and (c) in the circumstances, a reasonable person in the
-- position of the person would have taken the precautions." A flat negative with "unless": when a limb fails, s 9 bars
-- the finding (JUST FALSE kind). When all hold, s 9 does not say there is a breach; the court still decides (s 9(2)).
DECLARE `Can the court find a breach under s 9` IS ONE OF
    `s 9 does not stop the court finding a breach: the risk was foreseeable, not insignificant, and a reasonable person would have taken the precautions; the court decides`
    `no breach: the risk was not foreseeable (s 9(1)(a))`
    `no breach: the risk was insignificant (s 9(1)(b))`
    `no breach: a reasonable person in the defendant's position would not have taken the precautions (s 9(1)(c))`

GIVEN `foreseeable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not insignificant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a reasonable person would have taken them` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can the court find a breach under s 9`
`s 9 -- breach of a duty to take precautions` `foreseeable` `not insignificant` `a reasonable person would have taken them` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`foreseeable`) THEN `no breach: the risk was not foreseeable (s 9(1)(a))`
        IF NOT (`not insignificant`) THEN `no breach: the risk was insignificant (s 9(1)(b))`
        IF NOT (`a reasonable person would have taken them`) THEN `no breach: a reasonable person in the defendant's position would not have taken the precautions (s 9(1)(c))`
        OTHERWISE `s 9 does not stop the court finding a breach: the risk was foreseeable, not insignificant, and a reasonable person would have taken the precautions; the court decides`

@export Can the court find that the person I am suing breached their duty to take precautions against the risk that harmed me (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 9)? There is no breach unless the risk was foreseeable (known, or ought reasonably to have been known), was not insignificant, and a reasonable person in their position would have taken the precautions. Each of these is for the court; answer them as you expect the court to find.
GIVEN `foreseeable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk was foreseeable: one the person knew or ought reasonably to have known of
      `not insignificant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk was not insignificant
      `reasonable person would have taken precautions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a reasonable person in their position would have taken the precautions, weighing how probable and serious the harm was, the burden of the precautions and the social utility of the activity (s 9(2))
GIVETH A `Can the court find a breach under s 9`
`Can the court find a breach of duty` MEANS
    `s 9 -- breach of a duty to take precautions` `foreseeable` `not insignificant` `reasonable person would have taken precautions`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 1 div 3 -- obvious risks (ss 13-15) and inherent risks (s 16)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 13(1) "an obvious risk to a person who suffers harm is a risk that, in the circumstances, would have been obvious
-- to a reasonable person in the position of that person" (a judgment: asked). s 13(5) "a risk from a thing ... is not
-- an obvious risk if the risk is created because of a failure on the part of a person to properly operate, maintain,
-- replace, prepare or care for the thing, unless the failure itself is an obvious risk." A flat negative.
GIVEN `obvious to a reasonable person in my position` IS A BOOLEAN
      `created by a failure to operate, maintain, replace, prepare or care for a thing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `that failure was itself an obvious risk` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 13 -- an obvious risk` `obvious to a reasonable person in my position` `created by a failure to operate, maintain, replace, prepare or care for a thing` `that failure was itself an obvious risk` IF
        `obvious to a reasonable person in my position`
    AND (    (NOT (`created by a failure to operate, maintain, replace, prepare or care for a thing`))
         OR  `that failure was itself an obvious risk`)

-- s 15(1) "A person (defendant) does not owe a duty to another person (plaintiff) to warn of an obvious risk to the
-- plaintiff." (2) "Subsection (1) does not apply if-- (a) the plaintiff has requested advice or information about the
-- risk from the defendant; or (b) the defendant is required by a written law to warn the plaintiff of the risk; or (c)
-- the defendant is a professional, other than a doctor, and the risk is a risk of the death of or personal injury to
-- the plaintiff from the provision of a professional service by the defendant." (3) no presumption of a duty.
-- s 20 "a professional means a person practising a profession" (asked by choice).
DECLARE `Who the defendant is` IS ONE OF
    `a doctor`
    `a professional other than a doctor (a person practising a profession)`
    `not a professional`

DECLARE `Was I owed a warning of the risk` IS ONE OF
    `s 15 does not apply: the risk was not an obvious risk (s 13)`
    `no: the defendant owed me no duty to warn of an obvious risk (s 15(1))`
    `s 15(1) does not apply because I asked the defendant for advice or information about the risk (s 15(2)(a)); no duty to warn is presumed (s 15(3)), the general law decides`
    `s 15(1) does not apply because a written law required the defendant to warn me (s 15(2)(b)); no duty to warn is presumed (s 15(3)), the general law decides`
    `s 15(1) does not apply because the defendant is a professional, not a doctor, and the risk was of my death or personal injury from their professional service (s 15(2)(c)); no duty to warn is presumed (s 15(3)), the general law decides`

GIVEN `obvious` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I asked about the risk` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a written law required a warning` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the defendant` IS A `Who the defendant is`
      `a risk of death or injury from their professional service` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Was I owed a warning of the risk`
`s 15 -- duty to warn of an obvious risk` `obvious` `I asked about the risk` `a written law required a warning` `the defendant` `a risk of death or injury from their professional service` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`obvious`) THEN `s 15 does not apply: the risk was not an obvious risk (s 13)`
        IF `I asked about the risk`
        THEN `s 15(1) does not apply because I asked the defendant for advice or information about the risk (s 15(2)(a)); no duty to warn is presumed (s 15(3)), the general law decides`
        IF `a written law required a warning`
        THEN `s 15(1) does not apply because a written law required the defendant to warn me (s 15(2)(b)); no duty to warn is presumed (s 15(3)), the general law decides`
        IF (`the defendant` EQUALS `a professional other than a doctor (a person practising a profession)`) AND `a risk of death or injury from their professional service`
        THEN `s 15(1) does not apply because the defendant is a professional, not a doctor, and the risk was of my death or personal injury from their professional service (s 15(2)(c)); no duty to warn is presumed (s 15(3)), the general law decides`
        OTHERWISE `no: the defendant owed me no duty to warn of an obvious risk (s 15(1))`

@export Was the person I am suing obliged to warn me of the risk that harmed me, or does the Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 15 say there was no duty to warn of an obvious risk? An obvious risk is one that would have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position (s 13), but a risk created by someone's failure to maintain or care for a thing is not obvious unless that failure was itself obvious. There is no duty to warn of an obvious risk unless you asked about it, a written law required a warning, or the defendant is a professional other than a doctor and the risk was of death or injury from their professional service. For a doctor, see s 21.
GIVEN `obvious to a reasonable person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk would have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position, including a risk that is patent or a matter of common knowledge, even if unlikely or not physically observable (s 13(1)-(4))
      `created by a failure to maintain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk came from a thing (including an animal) and was created because someone failed to properly operate, maintain, replace, prepare or care for it (s 13(5))
      `the failure was itself obvious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only if it was: whether that failure was itself an obvious risk
      `I asked about the risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the defendant for advice or information about the risk
      `written law required a warning` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a written law required the defendant to warn you of the risk
      `defendant` IS A `Who the defendant is` @desc Who the defendant is: a doctor; a professional other than a doctor (a person practising a profession); or not a professional
      `risk from a professional service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a professional: whether the risk was a risk of your death or personal injury from the professional service they provided
GIVETH A `Was I owed a warning of the risk`
`Was I owed a warning of an obvious risk` MEANS
    `s 15 -- duty to warn of an obvious risk`
        (`s 13 -- an obvious risk` `obvious to a reasonable person` `created by a failure to maintain` `the failure was itself obvious`)
        `I asked about the risk` `written law required a warning` `defendant` `risk from a professional service`

-- s 14(1) "If ... a defence of voluntary assumption of risk is raised by the defendant and the risk is an obvious risk,
-- the plaintiff is taken to have been aware of the risk unless the plaintiff proves, on the balance of probabilities,
-- that he or she was not aware of the risk." (2) aware of the type or kind of risk is enough.
DECLARE `Am I taken to have known of the risk` IS ONE OF
    `s 14 does not apply: the defendant has not raised voluntary assumption of risk`
    `s 14 does not apply: the risk was not an obvious risk (s 13)`
    `yes: I am taken to have been aware of the risk, unless I prove I was not (s 14(1))`
    `no: I have proved on the balance of probabilities that I was not aware of the risk, even of its type or kind (s 14)`

GIVEN `the defence is raised` IS A BOOLEAN
      `obvious` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I prove I was not aware` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Am I taken to have known of the risk`
`s 14 -- presumed aware of an obvious risk` `the defence is raised` `obvious` `I prove I was not aware` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the defence is raised`) THEN `s 14 does not apply: the defendant has not raised voluntary assumption of risk`
        IF NOT (`obvious`) THEN `s 14 does not apply: the risk was not an obvious risk (s 13)`
        IF `I prove I was not aware` THEN `no: I have proved on the balance of probabilities that I was not aware of the risk, even of its type or kind (s 14)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: I am taken to have been aware of the risk, unless I prove I was not (s 14(1))`

@export If the person I am suing says I voluntarily accepted the risk, am I taken to have known about it (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 14)? Where the defendant raises voluntary assumption of risk and the risk was obvious, you are taken to have been aware of it unless you prove, on the balance of probabilities, that you were not. Being aware of the type or kind of risk is enough, even without knowing its precise nature, extent or manner.
GIVEN `defence raised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has raised the defence of voluntary assumption of risk (volenti non fit injuria)
      `obvious to a reasonable person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk would have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position (s 13)
      `created by a failure to maintain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk came from a thing and was created because someone failed to properly operate, maintain, replace, prepare or care for it (s 13(5))
      `the failure was itself obvious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only if it was: whether that failure was itself an obvious risk
      `I prove I was not aware` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove, on the balance of probabilities, that you were not aware of the risk, not even of its type or kind
GIVETH A `Am I taken to have known of the risk`
`Am I taken to have known of the risk` MEANS
    `s 14 -- presumed aware of an obvious risk` `defence raised`
        (`s 13 -- an obvious risk` `obvious to a reasonable person` `created by a failure to maintain` `the failure was itself obvious`)
        `I prove I was not aware`

-- s 16(1) "A person is not liable in negligence for harm suffered by another person as a result of the materialisation
-- of an inherent risk." (2) "An inherent risk is a risk of something occurring that can not be avoided by the exercise
-- of reasonable care and skill." (3) "This section does not operate to exclude liability in connection with a duty to
-- warn of a risk."
DECLARE `Does an inherent risk bar my claim` IS ONE OF
    `s 16 does not apply: the harm did not come from an inherent risk`
    `no liability in negligence: the harm came from an inherent risk, one that could not be avoided by reasonable care and skill (s 16(1))`
    `s 16 does not bar my claim that I should have been warned of the risk (s 16(3))`

GIVEN `could not be avoided by reasonable care and skill` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my claim is that I should have been warned` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Does an inherent risk bar my claim`
`s 16 -- inherent risk` `could not be avoided by reasonable care and skill` `my claim is that I should have been warned` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`could not be avoided by reasonable care and skill`) THEN `s 16 does not apply: the harm did not come from an inherent risk`
        IF `my claim is that I should have been warned` THEN `s 16 does not bar my claim that I should have been warned of the risk (s 16(3))`
        OTHERWISE `no liability in negligence: the harm came from an inherent risk, one that could not be avoided by reasonable care and skill (s 16(1))`

@export Can I recover damages in negligence for harm that came from an inherent risk (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 16)? An inherent risk is a risk of something occurring that cannot be avoided by the exercise of reasonable care and skill. No one is liable in negligence for harm from it materialising, but a claim that you should have been warned of the risk is not excluded.
GIVEN `unavoidable by reasonable care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the harm resulted from a risk that could not have been avoided by the exercise of reasonable care and skill
      `claim is for failure to warn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is that you should have been warned of the risk
GIVETH A `Does an inherent risk bar my claim`
`Does an inherent risk bar my claim` MEANS
    `s 16 -- inherent risk` `unavoidable by reasonable care` `claim is for failure to warn`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 1 div 4 -- dangerous recreational activities (ss 17-19)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 18 "dangerous recreational activity means an activity engaged in for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure that involves
-- a significant degree of risk of physical harm to a person." A "means" definition (the degree of risk is a judgment:
-- asked). s 17(1) "This division applies only in relation to liability in negligence for harm to a person resulting
-- from a dangerous recreational activity engaged in by the plaintiff." s 19 "(1) A person is not liable in negligence
-- for harm suffered by another person as a result of the materialisation of an obvious risk of a dangerous
-- recreational activity engaged in by the person suffering harm. (2) This section applies whether or not the person
-- suffering harm was aware of the risk."
DECLARE `Does a dangerous recreational activity bar my claim` IS ONE OF
    `s 19 does not apply: my claim is not in negligence (s 17(1))`
    `s 19 does not apply: the activity was not a dangerous recreational activity (s 18)`
    `s 19 does not apply: the harm did not come from an obvious risk of the activity materialising`
    `no liability in negligence: the harm came from an obvious risk of a dangerous recreational activity I engaged in, whether or not I was aware of it (s 19)`

GIVEN `in negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a significant degree of risk of physical harm` IS A BOOLEAN
      `obvious` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the harm came from that risk materialising` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Does a dangerous recreational activity bar my claim`
`s 19 -- dangerous recreational activity` `in negligence` `for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure` `a significant degree of risk of physical harm` `obvious` `the harm came from that risk materialising` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`in negligence`) THEN `s 19 does not apply: my claim is not in negligence (s 17(1))`
        IF NOT (`for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure` AND `a significant degree of risk of physical harm`)
        THEN `s 19 does not apply: the activity was not a dangerous recreational activity (s 18)`
        IF NOT (`obvious` AND `the harm came from that risk materialising`)
        THEN `s 19 does not apply: the harm did not come from an obvious risk of the activity materialising`
        OTHERWISE `no liability in negligence: the harm came from an obvious risk of a dangerous recreational activity I engaged in, whether or not I was aware of it (s 19)`

@export I was hurt doing a risky recreational activity. Does the Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 19 stop me suing in negligence? No one is liable in negligence for harm from an obvious risk of a dangerous recreational activity that you engaged in, whether or not you were aware of the risk. A dangerous recreational activity is one engaged in for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure that involves a significant degree of risk of physical harm.
GIVEN `claim in negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is in negligence (rather than, for example, only in contract or for breach of a statute)
      `for enjoyment relaxation or leisure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you engaged in the activity for enjoyment, relaxation or leisure
      `significant risk of physical harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the activity involved a significant degree of risk of physical harm to a person
      `obvious to a reasonable person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk that harmed you would have been obvious to a reasonable person in your position (s 13)
      `created by a failure to maintain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that risk came from a thing (for example equipment or an animal) and was created because someone failed to properly operate, maintain, replace, prepare or care for it (s 13(5))
      `the failure was itself obvious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only if it was: whether that failure was itself an obvious risk
      `harm from that risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your harm resulted from that risk materialising
GIVETH A `Does a dangerous recreational activity bar my claim`
`Does a dangerous recreational activity bar my claim` MEANS
    `s 19 -- dangerous recreational activity` `claim in negligence` `for enjoyment relaxation or leisure` `significant risk of physical harm`
        (`s 13 -- an obvious risk` `obvious to a reasonable person` `created by a failure to maintain` `the failure was itself obvious`)
        `harm from that risk`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 1 div 5 -- doctors and professionals (ss 20-22)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 21(1) "A doctor does not breach a duty owed to a patient to warn of risk ... unless the doctor at that time fails to
-- give or arrange to be given to the patient the following information about the risk-- (a) information that a
-- reasonable person in the patient's position would, in the circumstances, require to enable the person to make a
-- reasonably informed decision ...; (b) information that the doctor knows or ought reasonably to know the patient wants
-- to be given before making the decision". A flat negative with "unless".
DECLARE `Did my doctor breach the duty to warn` IS ONE OF
    `no breach of the duty to warn: the doctor gave me (or arranged) both kinds of information about the risk (s 21(1))`
    `s 21 does not stop a finding of breach: the doctor did not give the information a reasonable person in my position would require (s 21(1)(a))`
    `s 21 does not stop a finding of breach: the doctor did not give information the doctor knew or ought reasonably to have known I wanted (s 21(1)(b))`

GIVEN `gave what a reasonable person would require` IS A BOOLEAN
      `gave what the doctor knew or ought to have known I wanted` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Did my doctor breach the duty to warn`
`s 21 -- doctor's duty to warn` `gave what a reasonable person would require` `gave what the doctor knew or ought to have known I wanted` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`gave what a reasonable person would require`) THEN `s 21 does not stop a finding of breach: the doctor did not give the information a reasonable person in my position would require (s 21(1)(a))`
        IF NOT (`gave what the doctor knew or ought to have known I wanted`) THEN `s 21 does not stop a finding of breach: the doctor did not give information the doctor knew or ought reasonably to have known I wanted (s 21(1)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `no breach of the duty to warn: the doctor gave me (or arranged) both kinds of information about the risk (s 21(1))`

@export Did my doctor breach the duty to warn me of a risk of injury from treatment or advice (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 21)? A doctor does not breach the duty unless, before the treatment (or when giving the advice), the doctor failed to give or arrange for you the information a reasonable person in your position would need to make a reasonably informed decision, or information the doctor knew or ought reasonably to have known you wanted. For a patient under a legal disability, "you" includes the person responsible for the decision, such as a parent.
GIVEN `gave information a reasonable person would require` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the doctor gave you, or arranged for you to be given, the information about the risk that a reasonable person in your position would require to make a reasonably informed decision whether to undergo the treatment or follow the advice
      `gave information I wanted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the doctor gave you, or arranged for you to be given, the information about the risk that the doctor knew or ought reasonably to have known you wanted before deciding (answer yes if there was none)
GIVETH A `Did my doctor breach the duty to warn`
`Did my doctor breach the duty to warn me` MEANS
    `s 21 -- doctor's duty to warn` `gave information a reasonable person would require` `gave information I wanted`

-- s 22(1) "A professional does not breach a duty arising from the provision of a professional service if it is
-- established that the professional acted in a way that (at the time the service was provided) was widely accepted by
-- peer professional opinion by a significant number of respected practitioners in the field as competent professional
-- practice." (2) not if the court considers the opinion irrational or contrary to a written law. (5) not for
-- warnings, advice or information about the risk of harm.
DECLARE `Does peer professional opinion defend the professional` IS ONE OF
    `s 22 does not apply to a claim about a warning, advice or information about the risk of harm (s 22(5))`
    `s 22 gives no defence on these facts: it is not established that the professional acted as widely accepted peer professional opinion holds competent`
    `s 22 gives no defence on these facts: the court considers the peer professional opinion irrational or contrary to a written law (s 22(2))`
    `no breach: the professional acted in a way widely accepted by peer professional opinion as competent practice (s 22(1))`

GIVEN `about a warning or advice on risk` IS A BOOLEAN
      `acted as widely accepted peer opinion holds competent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the opinion is irrational or contrary to a written law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Does peer professional opinion defend the professional`
`s 22 -- peer professional opinion` `about a warning or advice on risk` `acted as widely accepted peer opinion holds competent` `the opinion is irrational or contrary to a written law` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `about a warning or advice on risk` THEN `s 22 does not apply to a claim about a warning, advice or information about the risk of harm (s 22(5))`
        IF NOT (`acted as widely accepted peer opinion holds competent`) THEN `s 22 gives no defence on these facts: it is not established that the professional acted as widely accepted peer professional opinion holds competent`
        IF `the opinion is irrational or contrary to a written law` THEN `s 22 gives no defence on these facts: the court considers the peer professional opinion irrational or contrary to a written law (s 22(2))`
        OTHERWISE `no breach: the professional acted in a way widely accepted by peer professional opinion as competent practice (s 22(1))`

@export Can the professional I am suing (for example a doctor, engineer or accountant) defeat my claim by showing other professionals would have done the same (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 22)? A professional does not breach a duty in providing a professional service if it is established that they acted in a way widely accepted, at the time, by a significant number of respected practitioners in the field as competent practice; the opinion need not be universal, and differing accepted opinions may each be relied on. It does not help them if the court considers the opinion irrational or contrary to a written law, or if your claim is about a warning, advice or information on the risk of harm.
GIVEN `claim about warning or advice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is about the giving of (or failure to give) a warning, advice or other information about the risk of harm
      `acted per accepted peer opinion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is established that the professional acted in a way that, when the service was provided, was widely accepted by peer professional opinion, by a significant number of respected practitioners in the field, as competent professional practice
      `opinion irrational or unlawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court considers that peer professional opinion irrational or contrary to a written law
GIVETH A `Does peer professional opinion defend the professional`
`Does peer professional opinion defend the professional` MEANS
    `s 22 -- peer professional opinion` `claim about warning or advice` `acted per accepted peer opinion` `opinion irrational or unlawful`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 1 div 7 -- first aid by public safety entities (ss 25-27; Civil Liability Regulation 2025 sch 1)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- ss 26(1), 27(1): "Civil liability does not attach to a person [or a prescribed entity] in relation to an act done or
-- omitted in the course of rendering first aid or other aid or assistance to a person in distress if-- (a) ... while
-- performing duties to enhance public safety for an entity prescribed under a regulation ...; (b) ... in circumstances
-- of emergency; and (c) the act is done or omitted in good faith and without reckless disregard for the safety of the
-- person in distress or someone else." Civil Liability Regulation 2025 s 4: "each entity mentioned in schedule 1 is
-- prescribed". Sch 1 names 21 entries, listed below in the words of the schedule (ABNs left out); the five helicopter
-- services are prescribed only "when providing aeromedical, air rescue or other emergency helicopter services under an
-- agreement with the State", and three entries take in "affiliated bodies". s 25 "person in distress includes" an injured, apparently
-- injured or at-risk person, or one suffering or apparently suffering an illness.
DECLARE `Who gave the aid` IS ONE OF
    `Babcock Mission Critical Services Australasia Pty Ltd when providing aeromedical, air rescue or other emergency helicopter services under an agreement with the State (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Brisbane City Council (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Capricorn Helicopter Rescue Service Limited when providing aeromedical, air rescue or other emergency helicopter services under an agreement with the State (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Central Queensland Helicopter Rescue Service Ltd when providing aeromedical, air rescue or other emergency helicopter services under an agreement with the State (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `the department in which the Disaster Management Act 2003 is administered (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `LifeFlight Australia Limited when providing aeromedical, air rescue or other emergency helicopter services under an agreement with the State (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `LifeFlight Retrieval Medicine Limited when providing aeromedical, air rescue or other emergency helicopter services under an agreement with the State (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `a local government or joint local government under the Local Government Act 2009 (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Marine Rescue Queensland established under the Marine Rescue Queensland Act 2024 (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Mediflite Limited (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Queensland Ambulance Service established under the Ambulance Service Act 1991 (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Queensland Fire and Rescue established under the Fire Services Act 1990 (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Queensland First Aid Volunteers Inc (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `the Queensland flotillas of The Australian Volunteer Coast Guard Association Incorporated (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Royal Flying Doctor Service of Australia (Queensland Section) Limited (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Royal Life Saving Society Queensland Inc and affiliated bodies (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Rural Fire Service Queensland established under the Fire Services Act 1990 (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `the State Emergency Service established under the State Emergency Service Act 2024 (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `St John Ambulance Australia Queensland Limited (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Surf Life Saving Queensland and affiliated bodies (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `Volunteer Marine Rescue Assoc Qld Inc and affiliated bodies (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it`
    `another entity or person, not prescribed in sch 1`

DECLARE `Can I sue for the aid given to me` IS ONE OF
    `no: civil liability does not attach to the aid given in an emergency, in good faith and without reckless disregard, by or for a prescribed public safety entity (ss 26, 27)`
    `ss 26 and 27 do not apply: the aid was not given by or for an entity prescribed by the Civil Liability Regulation 2025`
    `ss 26 and 27 do not apply: the aid was not given in circumstances of emergency`
    `ss 26 and 27 do not apply: the act was not done in good faith, or was done with reckless disregard for safety`

GIVEN `the giver` IS A `Who gave the aid`
      `while performing duties to enhance public safety` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an emergency` IS A BOOLEAN
      `in good faith and without reckless disregard` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I sue for the aid given to me`
`ss 26, 27 -- aid by a prescribed public safety entity` `the giver` `while performing duties to enhance public safety` `an emergency` `in good faith and without reckless disregard` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (`the giver` EQUALS `another entity or person, not prescribed in sch 1`) OR (NOT (`while performing duties to enhance public safety`))
        THEN `ss 26 and 27 do not apply: the aid was not given by or for an entity prescribed by the Civil Liability Regulation 2025`
        IF NOT (`an emergency`) THEN `ss 26 and 27 do not apply: the aid was not given in circumstances of emergency`
        IF NOT (`in good faith and without reckless disregard`) THEN `ss 26 and 27 do not apply: the act was not done in good faith, or was done with reckless disregard for safety`
        OTHERWISE `no: civil liability does not attach to the aid given in an emergency, in good faith and without reckless disregard, by or for a prescribed public safety entity (ss 26, 27)`

@export Can I sue over first aid or other help given to me in an emergency by a public safety service (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 26, 27; Civil Liability Regulation 2025 sch 1)? Civil liability does not attach to a prescribed entity, or a person performing public safety duties for it, for help given to a person in distress in an emergency, in good faith and without reckless disregard for safety. The Regulation's schedule 1 prescribes 21 entities, among them the Queensland Ambulance Service, Queensland Fire and Rescue, the Rural Fire Service, the State Emergency Service, Marine Rescue Queensland, any local government, the Royal Flying Doctor Service, St John Ambulance, Surf Life Saving and Royal Life Saving (with their affiliated bodies), the coast guard and volunteer marine rescue, and five helicopter rescue services when acting under an agreement with the State. Other rescuers may be protected by other laws (for example the Law Reform Act 1995, part 5), which this does not answer.
GIVEN `who gave the aid` IS A `Who gave the aid` @desc Who gave you first aid or other help: one of the 21 entities prescribed in schedule 1 of the Civil Liability Regulation 2025 (or a person performing duties for it), or another entity or person
      `public safety duties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the help was given while performing duties to enhance public safety for that entity
      `emergency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the help was given in circumstances of emergency
      `good faith without recklessness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or omission you complain of was done in good faith and without reckless disregard for your safety or anyone else's
GIVETH A `Can I sue for the aid given to me`
`Can I sue for emergency aid given to me` MEANS
    `ss 26, 27 -- aid by a prescribed public safety entity` `who gave the aid` `public safety duties` `emergency` `good faith without recklessness`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 2 -- proportionate liability (ss 28-32I) -- for claims for economic loss or damage to property`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 28(1) an apportionable claim is a claim for economic loss or damage to property in an action for damages arising
-- from a breach of a duty of care, or under the Fair Trading Act 1989 for a contravention of the Australian Consumer
-- Law (Queensland) s 18. (3) "This part does not apply to a claim-- (a) arising out of personal injury; or (b) by a
-- consumer." (4) nor to the extent an Act makes the liability joint and several.
-- s 29 "consumer means an individual whose claim is based on rights relating to goods or services, or both, in
-- circumstances where the particular goods or services-- (a) are being acquired for personal, domestic or household
-- use or consumption; or (b) relate to advice given by a professional to the individual for the individual's use,
-- other than for a business carried on by the individual ...". A "means" definition, worked out.
DECLARE `What my claim is based on` IS ONE OF
    `a breach of a duty of care`
    `misleading or deceptive conduct (Australian Consumer Law (Queensland) s 18, under the Fair Trading Act 1989)`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Is my claim apportionable` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my claim is an apportionable claim; each defendant who is a concurrent wrongdoer is liable only for its just share (s 31), and I am to make the claim against everyone I have reasonable grounds to believe may be liable (s 32(1))`
    `no: proportionate liability is only for claims for economic loss or damage to property (s 28(1))`
    `no: the claim is not for breach of a duty of care or under the Australian Consumer Law s 18 (s 28(1))`
    `no: proportionate liability does not apply to a claim arising out of personal injury (s 28(3)(a))`
    `no: proportionate liability does not apply to a claim by a consumer (s 28(3)(b), s 29)`
    `no: an Act makes the liability joint and several (s 28(4))`

GIVEN `I am an individual` IS A BOOLEAN
      `based on rights relating to goods or services` IS A BOOLEAN
      `acquired for personal, domestic or household use` IS A BOOLEAN
      `professional advice for my own use, not for my business` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 29 -- a consumer` `I am an individual` `based on rights relating to goods or services` `acquired for personal, domestic or household use` `professional advice for my own use, not for my business` IF
        `I am an individual`
    AND `based on rights relating to goods or services`
    AND (`acquired for personal, domestic or household use` OR `professional advice for my own use, not for my business`)

GIVEN `the harm` IS A `Kind of harm`
      `based on` IS A `What my claim is based on`
      `arising out of personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a consumer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `made joint and several by an Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Is my claim apportionable`
`s 28 -- an apportionable claim` `the harm` `based on` `arising out of personal injury` `a consumer` `made joint and several by an Act` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT ((`the harm` EQUALS `economic loss`) OR (`the harm` EQUALS `damage to property`))
        THEN `no: proportionate liability is only for claims for economic loss or damage to property (s 28(1))`
        IF `based on` EQUALS `something else`
        THEN `no: the claim is not for breach of a duty of care or under the Australian Consumer Law s 18 (s 28(1))`
        IF `arising out of personal injury` THEN `no: proportionate liability does not apply to a claim arising out of personal injury (s 28(3)(a))`
        IF `a consumer` THEN `no: proportionate liability does not apply to a claim by a consumer (s 28(3)(b), s 29)`
        IF `made joint and several by an Act` THEN `no: an Act makes the liability joint and several (s 28(4))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: my claim is an apportionable claim; each defendant who is a concurrent wrongdoer is liable only for its just share (s 31), and I am to make the claim against everyone I have reasonable grounds to believe may be liable (s 32(1))`

@export Is my claim for economic loss or property damage subject to proportionate liability, so that each wrongdoer pays only its share (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 28-32)? It applies to claims for economic loss or damage to property for breach of a duty of care, or for misleading or deceptive conduct under the Australian Consumer Law (Queensland) s 18, but not to a claim arising out of personal injury, a claim by a consumer, or a liability an Act makes joint and several. If it applies, you are to make the claim against everyone you have reasonable grounds to believe may be liable.
GIVEN `kind of harm` IS A `Kind of harm` @desc The kind of loss you claim for: economic loss, damage to property, personal injury, or another kind of harm
      `based on` IS A `What my claim is based on` @desc What your claim is based on: a breach of a duty of care; misleading or deceptive conduct under the Australian Consumer Law (Queensland) s 18; or something else
      `arises from personal injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim arises out of personal injury
      `individual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an individual (not a company or other body)
      `rights about goods or services` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is based on rights relating to goods or services
      `personal or household use` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those goods or services were being acquired for personal, domestic or household use or consumption
      `professional advice for personal use` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they relate to advice a professional gave you for your own use, not for a business you carry on
      `joint and several by an Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act provides that liability for the amount claimed is joint and several
GIVETH A `Is my claim apportionable`
`Is my claim apportionable` MEANS
    `s 28 -- an apportionable claim` `kind of harm` `based on` `arises from personal injury`
        (`s 29 -- a consumer` `individual` `rights about goods or services` `personal or household use` `professional advice for personal use`)
        `joint and several by an Act`

-- s 31(1) the liability of a defendant who is a concurrent wrongdoer "is limited to an amount reflecting that
-- proportion of the loss or damage claimed that the court considers just and equitable having regard to the extent of
-- the defendant's responsibility". s 32G: in apportioning between concurrent wrongdoers the court excludes "the
-- proportion of the damage or loss in relation to which the plaintiff is contributorily negligent". ss 32D-32F: a
-- concurrent wrongdoer found fraudulent, or to have intended the loss, or to have contravened ACL s 18, "is severally
-- liable for the damages awarded against any other concurrent wrongdoer" (fork F7: then the whole of the loss, less my
-- own share).
GIVEN `the loss` IS A NUMBER
      `my share` IS A NUMBER
      `the defendant's share` IS A NUMBER
      `fraud, intent or ACL s 18 found` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 31 -- the most I can recover from this defendant` `the loss` `my share` `the defendant's share` `fraud, intent or ACL s 18 found` MEANS
    IF `fraud, intent or ACL s 18 found`
    THEN `the part not mine`
    ELSE `the part not mine` TIMES (`the defendant's share` DIVIDED BY 100)
    WHERE
        `the part not mine` MEANS `the loss` TIMES ((100 MINUS `my share`) DIVIDED BY 100)

@export In a proportionate liability claim, what is the most I can get a judgment for against one wrongdoer (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 31, 32D-32G)? Each concurrent wrongdoer's liability is limited to the proportion of the loss the court considers just and equitable for its responsibility, after taking out the part for which you were contributorily negligent. A wrongdoer found to have been fraudulent, to have intended the loss, or to have engaged in misleading or deceptive conduct under the Australian Consumer Law s 18 is also liable for what is awarded against the others. Use this only where your claim is apportionable.
GIVEN `loss claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The loss or damage you claim, in dollars
      `my contributory negligence percent` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage of the loss for which you were contributorily negligent, as the court would find it (0 to 100; 0 if none)
      `defendant's share percent` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage of the remaining responsibility the court considers just and equitable for this defendant (0 to 100)
      `fraud intent or misleading conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this defendant is found to have been fraudulent, to have intended to cause the loss, or to have contravened the Australian Consumer Law (Queensland) s 18
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most I can recover from this wrongdoer` MEANS
    `s 31 -- the most I can recover from this defendant` `loss claimed` `my contributory negligence percent` `defendant's share percent` `fraud intent or misleading conduct`

-- s 32(2) "A concurrent wrongdoer, in relation to a claim involving an apportionable claim, must give the claimant any
-- information that the concurrent wrongdoer has-- (a) that is likely to help the claimant to identify and locate any
-- other person (not being a concurrent wrongdoer known to the claimant) who the concurrent wrongdoer has reasonable
-- grounds to believe is also a concurrent wrongdoer in relation to the claim; and (b) about the circumstances that make
-- the concurrent wrongdoer believe the other person is or may be a concurrent wrongdoer". (3) "in writing, as soon as
-- practicable after becoming aware of the claim being made or of the information, whichever is the later." (5) on
-- failure the court may on application, if just and equitable, order the wrongdoer "severally liable for any award of
-- damages made" or to "pay costs thrown away". (6) if the claimant joins a party on that information and it is found
-- not liable, the court may make costs orders. "As soon as practicable" fixes no day, so the duty is stated, not run as
-- a regulative rule; whether the wrongdoer has reasonable grounds is asked.
DECLARE `Must I be told of other wrongdoers` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the concurrent wrongdoer must give me, in writing, the information it has to help me identify and locate the other person, and why it believes that person is or may be a concurrent wrongdoer, as soon as practicable after it became aware of my claim or of the information, whichever is later (s 32(2), (3)); if it fails, the court may on application order it severally liable for any damages awarded, or to pay costs thrown away (s 32(5)); if I join the other person on that information and that person is found not liable, the court may make costs orders (s 32(6))`
    `no: s 32 applies only to a claim involving an apportionable claim (ss 28, 32(2))`
    `no: the duty is on a concurrent wrongdoer in relation to my claim (s 32(2))`
    `no: the duty is only about another person the wrongdoer has reasonable grounds to believe is also a concurrent wrongdoer, and who is not a concurrent wrongdoer known to me (s 32(2)(a))`
    `no: the wrongdoer has no information likely to help me identify and locate that person (s 32(2))`

GIVEN `apportionable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a concurrent wrongdoer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `believes on reasonable grounds another is a concurrent wrongdoer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `that other is already known to me` IS A BOOLEAN
      `has information to identify and locate them` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Must I be told of other wrongdoers`
`s 32(2)-(6) -- information about other wrongdoers` `apportionable` `a concurrent wrongdoer` `believes on reasonable grounds another is a concurrent wrongdoer` `that other is already known to me` `has information to identify and locate them` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`apportionable`) THEN `no: s 32 applies only to a claim involving an apportionable claim (ss 28, 32(2))`
        IF NOT (`a concurrent wrongdoer`) THEN `no: the duty is on a concurrent wrongdoer in relation to my claim (s 32(2))`
        IF (NOT (`believes on reasonable grounds another is a concurrent wrongdoer`)) OR `that other is already known to me`
        THEN `no: the duty is only about another person the wrongdoer has reasonable grounds to believe is also a concurrent wrongdoer, and who is not a concurrent wrongdoer known to me (s 32(2)(a))`
        IF NOT (`has information to identify and locate them`) THEN `no: the wrongdoer has no information likely to help me identify and locate that person (s 32(2))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the concurrent wrongdoer must give me, in writing, the information it has to help me identify and locate the other person, and why it believes that person is or may be a concurrent wrongdoer, as soon as practicable after it became aware of my claim or of the information, whichever is later (s 32(2), (3)); if it fails, the court may on application order it severally liable for any damages awarded, or to pay costs thrown away (s 32(5)); if I join the other person on that information and that person is found not liable, the court may make costs orders (s 32(6))`

@export In a proportionate liability claim, must a wrongdoer tell me about other people who may also be liable (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 32(2)-(6))? A concurrent wrongdoer must give you, in writing and as soon as practicable, any information it has that is likely to help you identify and locate another person it has reasonable grounds to believe is also a concurrent wrongdoer (not one you already know of), and why it believes so. If it does not, the court may make it liable for the whole award or for costs thrown away. You in turn are to claim against everyone you have reasonable grounds to believe may be liable (s 32(1)), and the court may make orders against you if you do not (s 32(4)).
GIVEN `claim apportionable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is an apportionable claim (a claim for economic loss or damage to property to which proportionate liability applies; see "Is my claim apportionable")
      `is a concurrent wrongdoer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you ask about is a concurrent wrongdoer: one of two or more people whose acts or omissions caused, independently or jointly, the loss or damage you claim (s 30)
      `believes another is liable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that wrongdoer has reasonable grounds to believe another person is also a concurrent wrongdoer in relation to your claim
      `other already known to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that other person is a concurrent wrongdoer already known to you
      `has identifying information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the wrongdoer has information likely to help you identify and locate that other person
GIVETH A `Must I be told of other wrongdoers`
`Must the wrongdoer tell me of other wrongdoers` MEANS
    `s 32(2)-(6) -- information about other wrongdoers` `claim apportionable` `is a concurrent wrongdoer` `believes another is liable` `other already known to me` `has identifying information`

-- s 32B(1) "nothing in this part prevents a plaintiff who has previously recovered judgment against a concurrent
-- wrongdoer for an apportionable part of any loss or damage from bringing another action against any other concurrent
-- wrongdoer for that loss or damage." (2) "However, ... the plaintiff can not recover an amount of damages that, having
-- regard to any damages previously recovered by the plaintiff in relation to the loss or damage, would result in the
-- plaintiff receiving compensation for loss or damage that is greater than the loss or damage actually suffered". The
-- most is the loss less what was recovered before, and never below nothing.
GIVEN `the loss actually suffered` IS A NUMBER
      `damages previously recovered` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 32B -- the most a later action can recover` `the loss actually suffered` `damages previously recovered` MEANS
    IF `damages previously recovered` GREATER THAN `the loss actually suffered`
    THEN 0
    ELSE `the loss actually suffered` MINUS `damages previously recovered`

@export After a judgment against one wrongdoer for its share, can I sue another wrongdoer, and for how much at most (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 32B)? Yes: nothing in the proportionate liability part stops you bringing another action against another concurrent wrongdoer for the same loss or damage. But you cannot recover damages that, with what you have already recovered for that loss, would compensate you for more than the loss you actually suffered. The answer is that ceiling in dollars; the court still decides that wrongdoer's own share (s 31). Use this only where your claim is apportionable.
GIVEN `loss actually suffered` IS A NUMBER @desc The loss or damage you actually suffered, in dollars
      `already recovered` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages you have already recovered for that loss or damage, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What more can I recover from another wrongdoer` MEANS
    `s 32B -- the most a later action can recover` `loss actually suffered` `already recovered`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 3 div 1 -- public and other authorities (ss 34-37)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 37(1) "A public or other authority is not liable in any legal proceeding for any failure by the authority in
-- relation to any function it has as a road authority-- (a) to repair a road or to keep a road in repair; or (b) to
-- inspect a road for the purpose of deciding the need to repair the road or to keep the road in repair." (2) "Subsection
-- (1) does not apply if at the time of the alleged failure the authority had actual knowledge of the particular risk
-- the materialisation of which resulted in the harm."
-- s 36(2) for a claim based on a wrongful exercise of, or failure to exercise, a function, the act or omission is not
-- wrongful "unless the act or omission was in the circumstances so unreasonable that no public or other authority
-- having the functions of the authority in question could properly consider the act or omission to be a reasonable
-- exercise of its functions."
DECLARE `What I complain the authority did` IS ONE OF
    `failed to repair a road or keep it in repair, or to inspect it for that purpose, as road authority`
    `wrongly exercised, or failed to exercise, another function`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Can I sue the public authority` IS ONE OF
    `no: a public or other authority is not liable for failing to repair or inspect a road, as it had no actual knowledge of the particular risk (s 37(1))`
    `s 37 does not bar the claim: the authority had actual knowledge of the particular risk that caused my harm (s 37(2))`
    `no: the act or omission was not so unreasonable that no authority with those functions could properly consider it reasonable, so it is not wrongful (s 36(2))`
    `s 36 does not bar the claim: the act or omission was so unreasonable that no authority could properly consider it a reasonable exercise of its functions (s 36(2))`
    `ss 36 and 37 do not apply; the court still applies the principles in s 35 about the authority's resources and functions`

GIVEN `the complaint` IS A `What I complain the authority did`
      `actual knowledge of the particular risk` IS A BOOLEAN
      `so unreasonable no authority could think it reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I sue the public authority`
`ss 36, 37 -- claims against public or other authorities` `the complaint` `actual knowledge of the particular risk` `so unreasonable no authority could think it reasonable` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the complaint`
    WHEN `failed to repair a road or keep it in repair, or to inspect it for that purpose, as road authority` THEN
        IF `actual knowledge of the particular risk`
        THEN `s 37 does not bar the claim: the authority had actual knowledge of the particular risk that caused my harm (s 37(2))`
        ELSE `no: a public or other authority is not liable for failing to repair or inspect a road, as it had no actual knowledge of the particular risk (s 37(1))`
    WHEN `wrongly exercised, or failed to exercise, another function` THEN
        IF `so unreasonable no authority could think it reasonable`
        THEN `s 36 does not bar the claim: the act or omission was so unreasonable that no authority could properly consider it a reasonable exercise of its functions (s 36(2))`
        ELSE `no: the act or omission was not so unreasonable that no authority with those functions could properly consider it reasonable, so it is not wrongful (s 36(2))`
    WHEN `something else` THEN `ss 36 and 37 do not apply; the court still applies the principles in s 35 about the authority's resources and functions`

@export Can I sue a council, the State or another public authority, for example over a road it did not repair (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 35-37)? An authority is not liable for failing to repair a road, keep it in repair, or inspect it for that purpose, unless it actually knew of the particular risk that caused your harm. A claim that it wrongly exercised, or failed to exercise, a function succeeds only if what it did was so unreasonable that no authority with its functions could properly consider it reasonable. In every case its resources and the broad range of its activities are taken into account (s 35).
GIVEN `complaint` IS A `What I complain the authority did` @desc What you say the authority did wrong: failed to repair or inspect a road as road authority; wrongly exercised or failed to exercise another of its functions; or something else
      `actual knowledge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a road: whether the authority, at the time of the failure, actually knew of the particular risk that materialised and harmed you
      `grossly unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another function: whether the act or omission was so unreasonable that no authority with those functions could properly consider it a reasonable exercise of them
GIVETH A `Can I sue the public authority`
`Can I sue the public authority` MEANS
    `ss 36, 37 -- claims against public or other authorities` `complaint` `actual knowledge` `grossly unreasonable`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 3 div 2 -- food donors and volunteers (ss 38-44)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 39(1) "A volunteer does not incur any personal civil liability in relation to any act or omission done or made by
-- the volunteer in good faith when doing community work-- (a) organised by a community organisation; or (b) as an
-- office holder of a community organisation." s 38: volunteer = an individual doing community work on a voluntary basis
-- (not under a court order; reimbursement of reasonable expenses is still voluntary). The protection is lost:
-- s 40 offence; s 41 intoxicated and failed to exercise due care and skill; s 42 knew or ought to have known acting
-- outside the authorised activities or contrary to instructions; s 43 insurance required by a written law; s 44 covered
-- by CTP insurance or recoverable from the Nominal Defendant.
DECLARE `Can I sue the volunteer personally` IS ONE OF
    `no: the volunteer incurs no personal civil liability for what they did in good faith doing community work for a community organisation (s 39(1)); the Act does not say here whether the organisation is liable`
    `s 39 does not protect: the person was not a volunteer doing community work on a voluntary basis (s 38)`
    `s 39 does not protect: the work was not organised by a community organisation, nor done as its office holder (s 39(1))`
    `s 39 does not protect: the act was not done in good faith (s 39(1))`
    `yes, the protection is lost: the volunteer was engaged in conduct that is an offence (s 40)`
    `yes, the protection is lost: the volunteer was intoxicated and failed to exercise due care and skill (s 41)`
    `yes, the protection is lost: the volunteer knew or ought to have known they were acting outside the authorised activities or contrary to instructions (s 42)`
    `yes, the protection is lost: a written law requires the volunteer to be insured against the liability (s 43)`
    `yes, the protection is lost: the liability is covered by CTP insurance or recoverable from the Nominal Defendant (s 44)`

GIVEN `a volunteer doing community work` IS A BOOLEAN
      `organised by a community organisation or as its office holder` IS A BOOLEAN
      `in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `intoxicated and without due care` IS A BOOLEAN
      `outside scope or against instructions, knowingly` IS A BOOLEAN
      `insurance required` IS A BOOLEAN
      `CTP or Nominal Defendant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I sue the volunteer personally`
`ss 39-44 -- the volunteer's protection` `a volunteer doing community work` `organised by a community organisation or as its office holder` `in good faith` `an offence` `intoxicated and without due care` `outside scope or against instructions, knowingly` `insurance required` `CTP or Nominal Defendant` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`a volunteer doing community work`) THEN `s 39 does not protect: the person was not a volunteer doing community work on a voluntary basis (s 38)`
        IF NOT (`organised by a community organisation or as its office holder`) THEN `s 39 does not protect: the work was not organised by a community organisation, nor done as its office holder (s 39(1))`
        IF NOT (`in good faith`) THEN `s 39 does not protect: the act was not done in good faith (s 39(1))`
        IF `an offence` THEN `yes, the protection is lost: the volunteer was engaged in conduct that is an offence (s 40)`
        IF `intoxicated and without due care` THEN `yes, the protection is lost: the volunteer was intoxicated and failed to exercise due care and skill (s 41)`
        IF `outside scope or against instructions, knowingly` THEN `yes, the protection is lost: the volunteer knew or ought to have known they were acting outside the authorised activities or contrary to instructions (s 42)`
        IF `insurance required` THEN `yes, the protection is lost: a written law requires the volunteer to be insured against the liability (s 43)`
        IF `CTP or Nominal Defendant` THEN `yes, the protection is lost: the liability is covered by CTP insurance or recoverable from the Nominal Defendant (s 44)`
        OTHERWISE `no: the volunteer incurs no personal civil liability for what they did in good faith doing community work for a community organisation (s 39(1)); the Act does not say here whether the organisation is liable`

@export Can I sue a volunteer personally for harm they caused me while volunteering (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 38-44)? A volunteer incurs no personal civil liability for acts done in good faith doing community work organised by a community organisation (such as a charity, church, club, school P&C or council), or as its office holder. The protection is lost if they were committing an offence, were intoxicated and careless, knowingly acted outside their authorised activities or against instructions, were required by law to be insured, or the liability is covered by compulsory third party motor insurance.
GIVEN `volunteer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was an individual doing community work (not for private financial gain, for a charitable, benevolent, philanthropic, sporting, recreational, political, educational or cultural purpose) on a voluntary basis; work under a court order is not voluntary, but being reimbursed reasonable expenses is
      `organised by community organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the work was organised (directed or supervised) by a community organisation, or done as an office holder of one
      `good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the volunteer acted in good faith
      `offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is established that, at the time, the volunteer was engaged in conduct that is an offence
      `intoxicated and careless` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the volunteer was intoxicated while doing the work and failed to exercise due care and skill
      `outside authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the volunteer knew or ought reasonably to have known they were acting outside the activities the organisation authorised, or contrary to its instructions
      `insurance required by law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Queensland written law requires the volunteer to be insured against this liability
      `CTP covered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the liability would be covered by a compulsory third party (CTP) motor insurance policy, or be recoverable from the Nominal Defendant, under the Motor Accident Insurance Act 1994
GIVETH A `Can I sue the volunteer personally`
`Can I sue the volunteer personally` MEANS
    `ss 39-44 -- the volunteer's protection` `volunteer` `organised by community organisation` `good faith` `offence` `intoxicated and careless` `outside authority` `insurance required by law` `CTP covered`

-- s 38A(1) "A food donor does not incur any civil liability ... when donating or distributing food in the circumstances
-- mentioned in subsection (2), giving rise to harm resulting from the consumption of the food." (2) the food was safe
-- when it left the donor's possession; any handling requirements and any time limit were told to the recipient.
-- s 38 a food donor does not include an entity that directly distributes the food to the consumer, or a volunteer.
-- s 38B, s 38C: not if insurance is required by a written law, or the liability is covered by CTP or the Nominal
-- Defendant. s 39(2), (3): a person donating food to a community organisation, in good faith for a charitable or like
-- purpose, intending the consumer not pay, is protected on the same food-safety conditions (and, being a "volunteer"
-- under s 38, loses it under ss 40-44; only s 43 and s 44 are asked here).
DECLARE `Who gave the food` IS ONE OF
    `a food donor that donated or distributed it in good faith for a charitable or similar purpose, free to the consumer, not handing it to me directly`
    `a person who donated it to a community organisation in good faith for a charitable or similar purpose, free to the consumer`
    `the one who handed it to me directly, or someone else`

DECLARE `Can I sue over donated food` IS ONE OF
    `no: the donor incurs no civil liability for harm from consuming the food, which was safe when it left their possession, with any handling needs and time limits passed on (ss 38A, 39(2))`
    `the protection does not apply: the person was not a food donor under s 38, nor a donor to a community organisation under s 39(3)`
    `the protection does not apply: the food was not safe to consume when it left the donor's possession`
    `the protection does not apply: the donor did not pass on the handling requirements or time limit the food needed`
    `the protection does not apply: a written law requires the donor to be insured against the liability (ss 38B, 43)`
    `the protection does not apply: the liability is covered by CTP insurance or recoverable from the Nominal Defendant (ss 38C, 44)`

GIVEN `the donor` IS A `Who gave the food`
      `safe when it left them` IS A BOOLEAN
      `handling and time limits passed on` IS A BOOLEAN
      `insurance required` IS A BOOLEAN
      `CTP or Nominal Defendant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I sue over donated food`
`ss 38A-38C, 39(2) -- food donors` `the donor` `safe when it left them` `handling and time limits passed on` `insurance required` `CTP or Nominal Defendant` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the donor` EQUALS `the one who handed it to me directly, or someone else`
        THEN `the protection does not apply: the person was not a food donor under s 38, nor a donor to a community organisation under s 39(3)`
        IF NOT (`safe when it left them`) THEN `the protection does not apply: the food was not safe to consume when it left the donor's possession`
        IF NOT (`handling and time limits passed on`) THEN `the protection does not apply: the donor did not pass on the handling requirements or time limit the food needed`
        IF `insurance required` THEN `the protection does not apply: a written law requires the donor to be insured against the liability (ss 38B, 43)`
        IF `CTP or Nominal Defendant` THEN `the protection does not apply: the liability is covered by CTP insurance or recoverable from the Nominal Defendant (ss 38C, 44)`
        OTHERWISE `no: the donor incurs no civil liability for harm from consuming the food, which was safe when it left their possession, with any handling needs and time limits passed on (ss 38A, 39(2))`

@export I became ill from donated food. Can I sue the donor (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 38A-38C, 39(2))? A business or other entity that donates or distributes food in good faith for a charitable or similar purpose, free to the consumer, and a person who donates food to a community organisation on those terms, incur no liability for harm from eating it if it was safe when it left them and they passed on any handling requirements and time limit it needed. The protection does not cover whoever handed the food to you directly, a liability a law requires to be insured, or one covered by CTP insurance.
GIVEN `donor` IS A `Who gave the food` @desc Who you want to sue: a food donor that gave or distributed the food in good faith for a charitable or similar purpose, free to the consumer, not handing it to you directly; a person who donated it to a community organisation on those terms; or the one who handed it to you directly, or someone else
      `safe when donated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the food was safe to consume when it left the donor's possession
      `handling and time limit passed on` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the donor told the recipient of any handling the food needed to stay safe, and of any time limit after which it was no longer safe (answer yes if it needed neither)
      `insurance required by law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Queensland written law requires the donor to be insured against this liability
      `CTP covered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the liability would be covered by a CTP motor insurance policy or recoverable from the Nominal Defendant
GIVETH A `Can I sue over donated food`
`Can I sue the food donor` MEANS
    `ss 38A-38C, 39(2) -- food donors` `donor` `safe when donated` `handling and time limit passed on` `insurance required by law` `CTP covered`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 4 div 1 -- harm suffered while committing an indictable offence (s 45)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 45(1) "A person does not incur civil liability if the court is satisfied on the balance of probabilities that--
-- (a) the breach of duty ... happened while the person who suffered harm was engaged in conduct that is an indictable
-- offence; and (b) the person's conduct contributed materially to the risk of the harm." (2) the court may still award
-- damages if satisfied (1) "would operate harshly and unjustly"; (3) then reduced by 25% or a greater percentage.
-- (4), (5) conviction, prosecution and summary disposal are immaterial.
DECLARE `Can I get damages although I was offending` IS ONE OF
    `s 45 does not bar my claim: I was not engaged in conduct that is an indictable offence when the breach happened`
    `s 45 does not bar my claim: my conduct did not contribute materially to the risk of the harm`
    `no: the defendant incurs no civil liability, because I was engaged in an indictable offence that contributed materially to the risk (s 45(1))`
    `yes, but reduced: the court finds s 45(1) would operate harshly and unjustly, and my damages are reduced by at least 25% (s 45(2), (3))`

GIVEN `engaged in an indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `contributed materially to the risk` IS A BOOLEAN
      `harsh and unjust` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I get damages although I was offending`
`s 45 -- harm while committing an indictable offence` `engaged in an indictable offence` `contributed materially to the risk` `harsh and unjust` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`engaged in an indictable offence`) THEN `s 45 does not bar my claim: I was not engaged in conduct that is an indictable offence when the breach happened`
        IF NOT (`contributed materially to the risk`) THEN `s 45 does not bar my claim: my conduct did not contribute materially to the risk of the harm`
        IF `harsh and unjust` THEN `yes, but reduced: the court finds s 45(1) would operate harshly and unjustly, and my damages are reduced by at least 25% (s 45(2), (3))`
        OTHERWISE `no: the defendant incurs no civil liability, because I was engaged in an indictable offence that contributed materially to the risk (s 45(1))`

@export I was hurt while I was doing something that is a serious (indictable) offence. Can I still get damages (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) s 45)? No one is civilly liable if the court is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that you were engaged in conduct that is an indictable offence when the breach happened and that your conduct contributed materially to the risk of the harm, whether or not you were charged or convicted. The court may still award damages if that would operate harshly and unjustly, but they are then cut by at least 25%.
GIVEN `indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied, on the balance of probabilities, that when the breach of duty happened you were engaged in conduct that is an indictable offence (whether or not you were charged, convicted or dealt with summarily)
      `contributed materially` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your conduct contributed materially to the risk of the harm
      `harsh and unjust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that barring your claim would operate harshly and unjustly in the circumstances of your case
GIVETH A `Can I get damages although I was offending`
`Can I get damages although I was committing an offence` MEANS
    `s 45 -- harm while committing an indictable offence` `indictable offence` `contributed materially` `harsh and unjust`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 4 div 2 -- intoxication (ss 46-49)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- Sch 2 "intoxicated ... means that the person is under the influence of alcohol or a drug to the extent that the
-- person's capacity to exercise proper care and skill is impaired." (asked)
-- s 47: if I was intoxicated at the time of the breach and the defendant alleges contributory negligence, it is
-- presumed; I may rebut it only by proving (a) the intoxication did not contribute to the breach, or (b) it was not
-- self-induced; otherwise damages are reduced by 25% or more (s 47(4)), at least 50% if I was the driver in a motor
-- vehicle accident with 150mg or more of alcohol in 100mL of blood, or so much under the influence as to be incapable of
-- effective control (s 47(5)).
-- s 48: if I was at least 16 at the time, relied on the care and skill of a person intoxicated at the time (the
-- defendant), and was or ought to have been aware of it, and the defendant alleges contributory negligence, it is
-- presumed; rebuttable only by proving (a) the defendant's intoxication did not contribute to the breach, or (b) I could
-- not reasonably be expected to have avoided relying on them; otherwise 25% or more (s 48(4)); volenti is not available
-- (s 48(5)). s 49: if it was a motor vehicle accident, I was a passenger in the vehicle driven by the defendant, and the
-- defendant had 150mg or more in 100mL of blood or was incapable of effective control, at least 50%.
-- s 24: the court may reduce by 100% if just and equitable.
DECLARE `How the minimum reduction arises` IS ONE OF
    `no presumption of contributory negligence arises under ss 47-49; any reduction is for the court under the general law (and may be up to 100%, s 24)`
    `contributory negligence is presumed and not rebutted: my damages are reduced by at least 25% (ss 47(4), 48(4))`
    `contributory negligence is presumed and not rebutted: my damages are reduced by at least 50%, the drink-driving minimum (ss 47(5), 49)`

-- s 47 for one plaintiff: the minimum percentage, or 0 where s 47 sets none.
GIVEN `I was intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN
      `contributory negligence alleged` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my intoxication did not contribute to the breach` IS A BOOLEAN
      `not self-induced` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was the driver in a motor vehicle accident` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my blood alcohol` IS A NUMBER
      `I was incapable of effective control` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 47 -- the minimum reduction for my own intoxication` `I was intoxicated` `contributory negligence alleged` `my intoxication did not contribute to the breach` `not self-induced` `I was the driver in a motor vehicle accident` `my blood alcohol` `I was incapable of effective control` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`I was intoxicated` AND `contributory negligence alleged`) THEN 0
        IF `my intoxication did not contribute to the breach` OR `not self-induced` THEN 0
        IF `I was the driver in a motor vehicle accident` AND ((`my blood alcohol` AT LEAST 150) OR `I was incapable of effective control`) THEN 50
        OTHERWISE 25

GIVEN `my age at the time` IS A NUMBER
      `I relied on an intoxicated defendant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was or ought to have been aware` IS A BOOLEAN
      `contributory negligence alleged` IS A BOOLEAN
      `their intoxication did not contribute to the breach` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I could not reasonably have avoided relying on them` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was their passenger in a motor vehicle accident` IS A BOOLEAN
      `their blood alcohol` IS A NUMBER
      `they were incapable of effective control` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`ss 48, 49 -- the minimum reduction for relying on an intoxicated person` `my age at the time` `I relied on an intoxicated defendant` `I was or ought to have been aware` `contributory negligence alleged` `their intoxication did not contribute to the breach` `I could not reasonably have avoided relying on them` `I was their passenger in a motor vehicle accident` `their blood alcohol` `they were incapable of effective control` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT ((`my age at the time` AT LEAST 16) AND `I relied on an intoxicated defendant` AND `I was or ought to have been aware`) THEN 0
        IF NOT (`contributory negligence alleged`) THEN 0
        IF `their intoxication did not contribute to the breach` OR `I could not reasonably have avoided relying on them` THEN 0
        IF `I was their passenger in a motor vehicle accident` AND ((`their blood alcohol` AT LEAST 150) OR `they were incapable of effective control`) THEN 50
        OTHERWISE 25

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the larger of` `a` `and` `b` MEANS IF `a` AT LEAST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

GIVEN `the minimum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `How the minimum reduction arises`
`ss 47-49 -- how the minimum arises` `the minimum` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the minimum` EQUALS 50 THEN `contributory negligence is presumed and not rebutted: my damages are reduced by at least 50%, the drink-driving minimum (ss 47(5), 49)`
        IF `the minimum` EQUALS 25 THEN `contributory negligence is presumed and not rebutted: my damages are reduced by at least 25% (ss 47(4), 48(4))`
        OTHERWISE `no presumption of contributory negligence arises under ss 47-49; any reduction is for the court under the general law (and may be up to 100%, s 24)`

@export By how much at least will my damages be cut because I was drunk or drugged, or relied on someone who was (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 47-49)? Answers the least percentage reduction the court must make (0 where no presumption arises). If you were intoxicated and the defendant alleges contributory negligence, it is presumed unless you prove your intoxication did not contribute to the breach or was not self-induced: at least 25%, or 50% if you were the driver with a blood alcohol of 150mg/100mL or more or incapable of control. If you were 16 or over and relied on someone you knew (or should have known) was intoxicated, the same applies unless their intoxication did not contribute or you could not reasonably have avoided relying on them: at least 25%, or 50% if you were their passenger and they were driving at 150mg/100mL or more or incapable of control.
GIVEN `I was intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were intoxicated at the time of the breach of duty: under the influence of alcohol or a drug to the extent that your capacity to exercise proper care and skill was impaired
      `contributory negligence alleged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant alleges contributory negligence on your part
      `my intoxication did not contribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove, on the balance of probabilities, that your intoxication did not contribute to the breach of duty
      `my intoxication not self-induced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that your intoxication was not self-induced
      `I was the driver` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a motor vehicle accident and you were the driver of a vehicle involved
      `my blood alcohol` IS A NUMBER @desc If you were the driver: the concentration of alcohol in your blood, in mg of alcohol per 100mL of blood (0 if none or not known)
      `I was incapable of control` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were the driver: whether you were so much under the influence of alcohol or a drug as to be incapable of exercising effective control of the vehicle
      `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years at the time of the breach of duty
      `I relied on an intoxicated person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you relied on the care and skill of a person (the defendant) who was intoxicated at the time of the breach
      `I knew or should have known` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were aware, or ought reasonably to have been aware, that they were intoxicated
      `their intoxication did not contribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that their intoxication did not contribute to the breach of duty
      `could not avoid relying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that you could not reasonably be expected to have avoided relying on their care and skill
      `I was their passenger` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a motor vehicle accident in which you were a passenger in the vehicle they drove
      `their blood alcohol` IS A NUMBER @desc If so: the concentration of alcohol in their blood, in mg per 100mL (0 if none or not known)
      `they were incapable of control` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether they were so much under the influence of alcohol or a drug as to be incapable of exercising effective control of the vehicle
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` MEANS
    `the larger of`
        (`s 47 -- the minimum reduction for my own intoxication` `I was intoxicated` `contributory negligence alleged` `my intoxication did not contribute` `my intoxication not self-induced` `I was the driver` `my blood alcohol` `I was incapable of control`)
        `and`
        (`ss 48, 49 -- the minimum reduction for relying on an intoxicated person` `my age` `I relied on an intoxicated person` `I knew or should have known` `contributory negligence alleged` `their intoxication did not contribute` `could not avoid relying` `I was their passenger` `their blood alcohol` `they were incapable of control`)

@export Why is there (or is there not) a minimum cut to my damages for intoxication (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 47-49)? Takes the same facts as the question about the least cut and explains which rule gives it. Where a presumption under s 48 arises, the defence of voluntary assumption of risk is not available against you (s 48(5)).
GIVEN `I was intoxicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were intoxicated at the time of the breach of duty: under the influence of alcohol or a drug to the extent that your capacity to exercise proper care and skill was impaired
      `contributory negligence alleged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant alleges contributory negligence on your part
      `my intoxication did not contribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove, on the balance of probabilities, that your intoxication did not contribute to the breach of duty
      `my intoxication not self-induced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that your intoxication was not self-induced
      `I was the driver` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a motor vehicle accident and you were the driver of a vehicle involved
      `my blood alcohol` IS A NUMBER @desc If you were the driver: the concentration of alcohol in your blood, in mg of alcohol per 100mL of blood (0 if none or not known)
      `I was incapable of control` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were the driver: whether you were so much under the influence of alcohol or a drug as to be incapable of exercising effective control of the vehicle
      `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years at the time of the breach of duty
      `I relied on an intoxicated person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you relied on the care and skill of a person (the defendant) who was intoxicated at the time of the breach
      `I knew or should have known` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were aware, or ought reasonably to have been aware, that they were intoxicated
      `their intoxication did not contribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that their intoxication did not contribute to the breach of duty
      `could not avoid relying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that you could not reasonably be expected to have avoided relying on their care and skill
      `I was their passenger` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a motor vehicle accident in which you were a passenger in the vehicle they drove
      `their blood alcohol` IS A NUMBER @desc If so: the concentration of alcohol in their blood, in mg per 100mL (0 if none or not known)
      `they were incapable of control` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether they were so much under the influence of alcohol or a drug as to be incapable of exercising effective control of the vehicle
GIVETH A `How the minimum reduction arises`
`Why are my damages cut for intoxication` MEANS
    `ss 47-49 -- how the minimum arises`
        (`What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` `I was intoxicated` `contributory negligence alleged` `my intoxication did not contribute` `my intoxication not self-induced` `I was the driver` `my blood alcohol` `I was incapable of control` `my age` `I relied on an intoxicated person` `I knew or should have known` `their intoxication did not contribute` `could not avoid relying` `I was their passenger` `their blood alcohol` `they were incapable of control`)

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 5 -- failed sterilisation or contraception (ss 49A, 49B)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- ss 49A(2), 49B(2): "A court can not award damages for economic loss arising out of the costs ordinarily associated
-- with rearing or maintaining a child." Applies where, after a sterilisation procedure, a contraceptive procedure or
-- contraceptive advice, the individual gives birth to, or fathers, a child because of a breach of duty in advising about
-- or performing it. A flat negative.
DECLARE `What failed` IS ONE OF
    `a sterilisation procedure (for example tubal ligation or vasectomy)`
    `a contraceptive procedure or contraceptive advice`
    `nothing of that kind`

DECLARE `Can I claim the costs of raising the child` IS ONE OF
    `no: a court cannot award damages for economic loss from the costs ordinarily associated with rearing or maintaining the child (s 49A(2) or 49B(2)); other damages are not affected by this section`
    `ss 49A and 49B do not apply: the child was not born or fathered after a failed sterilisation or contraceptive procedure or advice because of a breach of duty`

GIVEN `the failure` IS A `What failed`
      `because of a breach of duty` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I claim the costs of raising the child`
`ss 49A, 49B -- costs of rearing a child` `the failure` `because of a breach of duty` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`the failure` EQUALS `nothing of that kind`)) AND `because of a breach of duty`
    THEN `no: a court cannot award damages for economic loss from the costs ordinarily associated with rearing or maintaining the child (s 49A(2) or 49B(2)); other damages are not affected by this section`
    ELSE `ss 49A and 49B do not apply: the child was not born or fathered after a failed sterilisation or contraceptive procedure or advice because of a breach of duty`

@export After a failed sterilisation, contraceptive procedure or contraceptive advice I had (or fathered) a child. Can I claim the costs of raising the child (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 49A, 49B)? Where the child was born or fathered because of a breach of duty in advising about or performing the procedure or giving the advice, a court cannot award damages for economic loss arising out of the costs ordinarily associated with rearing or maintaining the child.
GIVEN `what failed` IS A `What failed` @desc What failed: a sterilisation procedure (for example tubal ligation or vasectomy); a contraceptive procedure or contraceptive advice; or nothing of that kind
      `breach of duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave birth to, or fathered, the child because of a breach of duty of the person who advised about or performed the procedure or gave the advice
GIVETH A `Can I claim the costs of raising the child`
`Can I claim the costs of raising the child` MEANS
    `ss 49A, 49B -- costs of rearing a child` `what failed` `breach of duty`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 4 pts 1, 1A -- expressions of regret and apologies (ss 68-72D)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 72 "An expression of regret made by an individual in relation to an incident alleged to give rise to an action for
-- damages at any time before a civil proceeding is started in a court in relation to the incident is not admissible in
-- the proceeding." s 71: only "to the extent that it does not contain an admission of liability". s 68: personal
-- injury damages claims only.
-- s 72D "(1) An apology made by or on behalf of a person in relation to any matter alleged to have been caused by the
-- person-- (a) does not constitute an express or implied admission of fault or liability ...; and (b) is not relevant
-- to the determination of fault or liability ... (2) Evidence of an apology made by a person is not admissible in any
-- civil proceeding as evidence of the fault or liability of the person in relation to the matter." s 72C: an apology
-- is "an expression of sympathy or regret, or of a general sense of benevolence or compassion ..., whether or not it
-- admits or implies an admission of fault". s 72A(2): part 1A does not apply to liability excluded by s 5, to
-- defamation, to an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury, or to an unlawful sexual
-- assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct; (3) but it does apply to an apology by or for an institution about the
-- abuse of a child by a person associated with it.
DECLARE `What the claim is about` IS ONE OF
    `an ordinary claim for harm`
    `a personal injury claim the Act does not apply to under s 5 (for example workers' compensation, dust disease or tobacco)`
    `defamation`
    `an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury`
    `an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct`

DECLARE `Can the apology be used against the defendant` IS ONE OF
    `no: the apology is not an admission of fault or liability, is not relevant to deciding fault or liability, and is not admissible as evidence of it (s 72D)`
    `no: the expression of regret, made before the proceeding started, is not admissible in the proceeding (s 72), except any part that admits liability (s 71)`
    `the Act does not stop it: neither part 1 nor part 1A of chapter 4 covers this statement`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A `What the claim is about`
      `personal injury damages` IS A BOOLEAN
      `by an institution about child abuse` IS A BOOLEAN
      `made before the proceeding started` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an expression of regret by an individual` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can the apology be used against the defendant`
`ss 72, 72A-72D -- regret and apologies` `the claim` `personal injury damages` `by an institution about child abuse` `made before the proceeding started` `an expression of regret by an individual` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (`the claim` EQUALS `an ordinary claim for harm`)
           OR (`by an institution about child abuse` AND ((`the claim` EQUALS `an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury`) OR (`the claim` EQUALS `an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct`)))
        THEN `no: the apology is not an admission of fault or liability, is not relevant to deciding fault or liability, and is not admissible as evidence of it (s 72D)`
        IF `personal injury damages` AND `made before the proceeding started` AND `an expression of regret by an individual` AND (NOT (`the claim` EQUALS `a personal injury claim the Act does not apply to under s 5 (for example workers' compensation, dust disease or tobacco)`))
        THEN `no: the expression of regret, made before the proceeding started, is not admissible in the proceeding (s 72), except any part that admits liability (s 71)`
        OTHERWISE `the Act does not stop it: neither part 1 nor part 1A of chapter 4 covers this statement`

@export The person I am suing said sorry. Can I use the apology as evidence that they were at fault (Civil Liability Act 2003 (Qld) ss 68-72D)? An apology (an expression of sympathy, regret or compassion, even one admitting fault) is not an admission and is not admissible as evidence of fault or liability, except in claims for defamation, for an unlawful intentional act done to cause injury, or for unlawful sexual assault or misconduct (but it still protects an institution apologising for child abuse), and in personal injury claims the Act does not apply to. For a personal injury claim, an individual's expression of regret made before the proceeding started is also inadmissible, so far as it does not admit liability.
GIVEN `claim about` IS A `What the claim is about` @desc What your claim is about: an ordinary claim for harm; a personal injury claim the Act does not apply to under s 5 (workers' compensation payable, dust disease or tobacco); defamation; an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury; or an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct
      `personal injury claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for personal injury damages (damages for death or injury)
      `institution apology for child abuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the apology was made by or for an institution, about the abuse of a child by a person associated with it
      `before proceeding started` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made before a court proceeding about the incident was started
      `regret by an individual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made by an individual and expressed regret for the incident
GIVETH A `Can the apology be used against the defendant`
`Can the apology be used against the defendant` MEANS
    `ss 72, 72A-72D -- regret and apologies` `claim about` `personal injury claim` `institution apology for child abuse` `before proceeding started` `regret by an individual`
